WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.430
Film Courage: How important is the first kiss
in a romantic comedy?

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:18.820
Pamela Jaye Smith: It is…maybe…the…MOST
important moment followed closely by the one

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:24.720
towards the end where the two people realize
and admit to each other their love for each

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.270
other.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.580
But that first kiss, wow that’s amazing!

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:37.930
That’s when you’re standing on the threshold
of paradise and that’s when you know whether

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.050
or not if it’s going to work.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:48.379
Because if that kiss does not transport your
characters, it does not turn on all the lights

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:53.579
and all the bells and whistles and the fireworks
and all that people talk about in poetry and

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:59.220
literature for thousands and thousands of
years, if that’s not there, there is no

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.969
story, there is no story.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:07.709
So building up to the first kiss, you need
to have that tension, that push-pull so that

00:01:07.709 --> 00:01:14.850
when you get there and the positive and the
negative come together, sparks do fly.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.259
Film Courage: I’m thinking of [the movie]
PRETTY WOMAN.

00:01:18.259 --> 00:01:20.689
So there’s the implication of this.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:27.549
There’s definitely the agreement the sort
of business arrangement, but I’m trying

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:33.149
to remember does that actually happen until
they have the first kiss and feel something

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:34.149
for one another?

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:37.599
Pamela Jaye Smith: That’s when it happens,
at that first kiss.

00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:42.929
Now I think they’ve been feeling a comradeship,
they’ve been feeling admiration, they’ve

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:48.490
been having fun, they’ve been finding that
they do agree on some things and not others,

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.609
and so there is an affinity, there’s definitely
an affinity.

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:56.100
And you’re right, they presumably have been
having relations.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.049
And she’s a pro, so okay.

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:01.950
You figure it’s got to be pretty good.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:11.510
But that moment of actually kissing is when
the magic really starts to happen.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:16.019
Now remember, she was a professional.

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:23.540
There is a reason why people who are workers,
there is no kissing allowed because that creates

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:35.379
let’s say an energetic, electrical, etheric,
esoteric connection between people that really

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:37.640
does something, it can change everything.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.659
Film Courage: It seems like two with those
two characters [in the movie PRETTY WOMAN

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:45.599
- Vivian and Edward] once they got out of
their roles, because his role was of “Here,

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:46.719
I’m going to pay you.

00:02:46.719 --> 00:02:51.159
You are kind of beneath me and I’m this
prestigious business man.”

00:02:51.159 --> 00:02:53.049
And she’s like “Who do you think you are?”

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:55.569
And “I’ve seen guys like you, you’re
a dime a dozen.”

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:01.420
But once they got out of those identities
and they became real, that’s when it seemed

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.530
like the real kiss and the real relationship
happened because they stopped being in their

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.530
element.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:12.140
Pamela Jaye Smith: Yeah, that’s a good point,
when they just became themselves and not the

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.560
masks and not the costumes.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.299
Film Courage: I like that.

00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:21.990
Pamela Jaye Smith: And there’s a wonderful
line in JERRY MAGUIRE before they meet the

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.459
first time.

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:27.590
And he says “You know, this changes everything?”

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.420
And she says “Oh, I hope so!”

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:35.030
Film Courage: And what I love is the sister
in JERRY MAGUIRE [Actress Bonnie Hunt as Laurel

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.299
Boyd] and I want to talk about that…the
sister in JERRY MAGUIRE and that wonderful

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:44.049
scene where she’s having her women’s group
[in the living room] and he comes in and there

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:50.019
are a few women in the group that look at
him [Tom Cruise as Jerry Maguire] but there

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:52.319
are just dagger pointed his way.

00:03:52.319 --> 00:04:02.420
And then the Jiminy Cricket friend [in most
romantic comedies], I want to talk about that

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:07.970
role, the third person who is either trying
to talk them into getting together or separate

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:08.970
them.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:13.280
Pamela Jaye Smith: When I first started selecting
films for this book [Romantic Comedies: These

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.810
Films Can Save Your Love Life], Ken Lee, the
publisher [Michael Wiese Productions] said

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.769
“You have to include TRAINWRECK.”

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.190
And I went “TRAINWRECK?

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.430
I wouldn’t even go see that.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:22.440
Why would I…?

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.440
No!

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.289
This is about people’s relationships getting
better.

00:04:25.289 --> 00:04:27.270
You don’t want to see TRAINWRECK.”

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.720
And he said “Go see TRAINWRECK.”

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.280
I could not believe it.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.460
It’s really good!

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:39.020
And it has in it the qualities of joy and
dignity and passion and integrity.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.919
I was so surprised.

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:46.490
Now one of the reasons it does it because
the Jiminy Cricket character, the best friend

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.320
is LeBron James, okay!

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.979
That works!

00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:58.599
And he is best friends with Bill Hader and
sees something in Amy Schumer.

00:04:58.599 --> 00:05:01.830
He sees what she can be.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.710
And he convinces the two of them to get back
together.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.490
And to admit their feelings for each other.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:14.600
So that’s a wonderful instance of that different
kind of love triangle (that you’ve pointed

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.960
out), where you’ve got the lovers and then
that third person who helps them see what

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.069
it is that they have or what they could have.

00:05:22.069 --> 00:05:26.580
Film Courage: Let’s say when that third
person is actually trying to prevent them

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.361
from being together because…like if you
take PRETTY WOMAN and I forget his name [in

00:05:30.361 --> 00:05:34.979
the movie] but George Constanza’s character
on SEINFELD, the one that is Edward’s business

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:36.919
partner.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:42.160
And he’s kind of this negative force and
antagonizing them.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.389
So that third person as the one who is trying
to prevent them from being together?

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:53.100
Pamela Jaye Smith: Yes, you need the third
person either helping them because they they’re

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.349
not wanting to get together or (as you described)
trying to keep them apart.

00:05:58.349 --> 00:06:02.370
Well you need that for dramatic tension.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:08.680
And also we need the secondary characters
to help tell us things about the main characters

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.210
so they can make those statements.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.870
You know when you say “Oh, I can’t have
him saying that?

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.740
He’d never say that about himself.”

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.720
Well give that line to his friend.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:20.659
Or give the line to her friend.

00:06:20.659 --> 00:06:23.289
Who would say “Ahh!

00:06:23.289 --> 00:06:27.460
Have you no idea what this guy is like?”

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:34.020
So the third character is very, very important
in any script.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.470
All of the secondary characters need somehow
to be showing us something about the main

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.090
characters or they don’t need to be there.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:49.599
Now whether it’s they are people that the
main characters do something to or with or

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:55.379
for, but they are there to service the main
characters in the storytelling.

